<section class="section wrap">
  <p class="kicker">How the pieces can work together</p>
  <h2>One strategy, with different jobs to do.</h2>
  <p>A 401(k) can provide familiar employee savings features and flexibility. A Cash Balance Plan can create additional defined benefit capacity for owners who have the cash flow and timeline to support it.</p>
  <div class="grid3" style="margin-top:22px;">
    <article class="panel"><p class="kicker">01</p><h3>401(k)</h3><p>A familiar defined contribution foundation for employee deferrals, employer contributions, and day-to-day savings.</p></article>
    <article class="panel"><p class="kicker">02</p><h3>Cash Balance</h3><p>An additional retirement layer designed around a targeted benefit and the owner’s age, income, and funding objective.</p></article>
    <article class="panel"><p class="kicker">03</p><h3>Connected administration</h3><p>A coordinated process that keeps plan documents, census data, testing, and communications aligned.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt"><div class="wrap split">
  <div>
    <p class="kicker">Questions to bring to the table</p>
    <h2>The right combination depends on the whole picture.</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>How stable is your business cash flow?</li>
      <li>What do you already contribute to a retirement plan?</li>
      <li>Who is eligible for each arrangement?</li>
      <li>How many years do you expect to fund the plan?</li>
      <li>Which advisors should be part of the decision?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <img class="photo" src="/images/plans.jpg" alt="Contribution comparison visual" style="height:380px;" />
